2.text:
An air ionisation chamber gives the air kerma rate in the beam by

* $W/e=33.97\;{\rm V}$ in air, so that the power deposited in the air
in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
 \dot E = I\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

with $I=2.0\times10^{-12}\;{\rm A}$,

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =2.0\times10^{-12}\times 33.97=6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}\;,
\end{equation*}

of ionising energy deposited in the air which is actually illuminated by
the 30 $\mu$m $\times$ 6 mm focus.

The mass of air which is in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=w_{f}h_{f}L_{c}
     =0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}\times151\,{\rm mm}
     =2.72\times10^{-1}\,{\rm cm^{3}} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{b}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}\approx 3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

so that the instantaneous air kerma rate in the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{a}}
       =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}V_{b}}
       \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
       \approx 1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air, and with $\mu_{en}/\rho_{tissue}\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$
the same absorbed dose rate is obtained in soft tissue for the same
photon fluence,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx 1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,min^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

In the scanning geometry a material point is under the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm
mm}$ focal spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}\approx 0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the “beam width/scan speed’’ quoted in the problem.  Hence the absorbed
dose which is delivered to a surface element which is traversed once by
the focused 33 keV beam is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
\approx 3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy and per pass, i.e. of the order of a few $\mu$Gy, $\approx$
$0.4$ mrad, at the tissue surface in the focus.  With the usual
$\mu_{en}/\rho$ equivalence

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \Phi E\,\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
\qquad
\Phi\approx1.1\times10^{12} {\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

in the 0.3 mm$\times$6 mm focus.  In practice the multiple-energy
acquisition is $N_{E}$ times this value, e.g.

* $N_{E}=2$: $D_{2}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$, 
* $N_{E}=3$: $D_{3}\simeq11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$,

per scan line, i.e. essentially a few $\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}$ per
projection, essentially negligible for diagnostic purposes.  The 50 mm
$\times$ 6 mm illumination on the Laue crystal is the total field which
is swept out by the 0.3 mm focus; the average dose over the whole
swept field is the dwell dose quoted above.  This is the often quoted
$\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV projection for the present
synchrotron multi-energy set-up.

5.text:
In the air the charge which is produced by the photons is

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q=I=2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm C\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
N_{i}=\dot Q/e
\end{equation*}
ion pairs are produced per second.  With $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\frac{W}{e}=I\,W_{air}
\end{equation*}
is the energy which is put into the gas in the illuminated volume.
The volume which is illuminated at any instant is the volume which is
common to the focussed beam and to the air in the chamber

\begin{equation*}
V_{i}=A_{f}\,L_{c}\;,
\qquad 
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{i}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}\times 15.1{\rm cm}=2.72\times10^{-1}
{\rm cm^{3}} .
\end{equation*}

Hence

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{i}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
\times 2.72\times10^{-1}{\rm cm^{3}}\approx 3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant.  The air
kerma rate in the beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{I\,W_{air}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97}{3.51\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e.  $\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air in the focussed beam.
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the same
number is the absorbed-dose rate in soft tissue in the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the focussed 0.3$\times$6 mm$^{2}$ spot.

A material point of the object is in the beam for the time it takes the
scan to move one beam width past it,

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}
which is the quantity quoted in the statement.  With the value quoted

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dose actually deposited in one dwell, i.e. the dose deposited in
one pixel of the multiple energy scan, is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq\dot D_{t}\,t_{d}
       \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times 2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
       \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, per pixel in the 33 keV
focussed spot.  This is the conventional "surface" dose of the
synchrotron micro-beam; in air kerma units it is $\simeq4.4\,\mu{\rm R}$
per dwell.

If the dose is integrated over the length of chamber which is
normally scanned, $L_{c}=15.1\;{\rm cm}$, the time the whole length is
exposed at the scan speed $v_{s}=w_{f}/t_{d}=0.30\,{\rm mm}/0.02\,{\rm s}
=15\,{\rm mm\,s^{-1}}$ is $T_{L}=L_{c}/v_{s}\approx10\,{\rm s}$ and the
energy which is deposited in the scanned $L_{c}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ strip
is

\begin{equation*}
D_{L}\simeq\dot D_{t}T_{L}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\times 10.1\approx
1.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm Gy}\approx2\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

in the $15.1{\rm cm}\times0.6{\rm cm}$ strip, i.e. $\approx\!4\;\mu{\rm
Gy}$ per $0.3{\rm mm}$ pixel, $ \approx 0.04\,{\rm mGy\,cm^{-1}}$ of
scan.  The $4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per dwell is the usual quoted
synchrotron multi-energy micro-CT surface dose; for $n_{E}$ energies the
dose scales as $n_{E}D_{1}$, e.g. $\simeq12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the
common three-energy set.  This is the $\sim {\rm \mu Gy}$–level surface
dose normally quoted for Laue-monochromator synchrotron micro-imaging.

